# Hidden Markov Models {#hidden-markov-models}

This section works through Questions 1 to 7 of the HMM tutorial. It uses the HMM toolkit (scaled forward and backward algorithms, local decoding, the Viterbi algorithm and the stationary distribution) that I wrote from first principles for [Project 4](../projects/project-04-analysis.qmd), where the toolkit is explained in detail. Question 8, Poisson HMMs for the earthquake counts, is in Project 4, because it is where I checked the toolkit against `depmixS4` before applying it to JSE returns.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(tidyverse)

# Same seed and colours as in Project 4:
set.seed(2026)
col_main   = "purple"
col_accent = "deeppink"
state_cols = c("1" = "steelblue", "2" = "purple", "3" = "deeppink", "4" = "black")
```
:::

::: {.cell file='scripts/hmm-toolkit.R'}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="HMM toolkit (scripts/hmm-toolkit.R)"}
# =============================================================================
# HMM toolkit, written from first principles (Time Series Analysis portfolio)
#
# Used by projects/project-04-analysis.qmd and section-2/section-2.qmd.
# Every function works with the matrix of state-dependent densities
# dens[t, i] = p_i(x_t), so the same code handles Poisson, Gaussian and
# categorical observations.
# =============================================================================

# Log-likelihood only (fast version, used inside optim()):
hmm_loglik = function(dens, delta, Gamma) {
  T_len = nrow(dens)
  v = delta * dens[1, ]
  ll = log(sum(v))
  phi = v / sum(v)
  if (T_len > 1) {
    for (t in 2:T_len) {
      v = as.numeric(phi %*% Gamma) * dens[t, ]
      u = sum(v)
      ll = ll + log(u)
      phi = v / u
    }
  }
  ll
}

# Scaled forward algorithm: log-likelihood, log u_t, filtered probabilities and log alpha:
hmm_forward = function(dens, delta, Gamma) {
  T_len = nrow(dens)
  m = ncol(dens)
  phi_mat = matrix(NA, T_len, m)
  lu = numeric(T_len)
  for (t in 1:T_len) {
    v = if (t == 1) delta * dens[1, ] else as.numeric(phi %*% Gamma) * dens[t, ]
    u = sum(v)
    lu[t] = log(u)                # log p(x_t | x_{1:t-1})
    phi = v / u                   # P(C_t = i | x_{1:t})
    phi_mat[t, ] = phi
  }
  list(loglik = sum(lu), lu = lu, phi = phi_mat,
       lalpha = cumsum(lu) + log(phi_mat))   # log alpha_t(i)
}

# Scaled backward algorithm: log beta (Zucchini et al., 2016):
hmm_backward = function(dens, Gamma) {
  T_len = nrow(dens)
  m = ncol(dens)
  lbeta = matrix(0, T_len, m)     # log beta_T = 0
  phi = rep(1 / m, m)
  lscale = log(m)
  if (T_len > 1) {
    for (t in (T_len - 1):1) {
      v = as.numeric(Gamma %*% (dens[t + 1, ] * phi))
      lbeta[t, ] = log(v) + lscale
      phi = v / sum(v)
      lscale = lscale + log(sum(v))
    }
  }
  lbeta
}

# Local decoding: P(C_t = i | x_{1:T}) = alpha_t(i) beta_t(i) / L_T:
hmm_local = function(dens, delta, Gamma) {
  fwd = hmm_forward(dens, delta, Gamma)
  lbeta = hmm_backward(dens, Gamma)
  exp(fwd$lalpha + lbeta - fwd$loglik)
}

# Global decoding with the Viterbi algorithm (log scale):
hmm_viterbi = function(dens, delta, Gamma) {
  T_len = nrow(dens)
  m = ncol(dens)
  lG = log(Gamma)
  ld = log(dens)
  xi = matrix(NA, T_len, m)
  xi[1, ] = log(delta) + ld[1, ]
  if (T_len > 1) {
    for (t in 2:T_len) {
      for (j in 1:m) {
        xi[t, j] = max(xi[t - 1, ] + lG[, j]) + ld[t, j]
      }
    }
  }
  # Back-tracing:
  states = numeric(T_len)
  states[T_len] = which.max(xi[T_len, ])
  if (T_len > 1) {
    for (t in (T_len - 1):1) {
      states[t] = which.max(xi[t, ] + lG[, states[t + 1]])
    }
  }
  list(states = states, xi = xi)
}

# Stationary distribution: solve delta (I - Gamma + U) = 1:
stat_dist = function(Gamma) {
  m = nrow(Gamma)
  solve(t(diag(m) - Gamma + 1), rep(1, m))
}

# Working (unconstrained) parameters -> transition probability matrix:
gamma_w2n = function(w, m) {
  G = diag(m)
  G[!G] = exp(w)                  # off-diagonal elements, relative to the diagonal
  G / rowSums(G)
}

# h-step transition matrix Gamma^h:
mat_pow = function(M, h) {
  R = diag(nrow(M))
  for (i in seq_len(h)) R = R %*% M
  R
}
```
:::


## Question 1: Simulating a three-state bivariate Gaussian HMM


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# State-dependent means and covariance matrices (sigma must be created first):
mu_q1 = 10 * matrix(c(-1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1), ncol = 2, byrow = TRUE)
sf = 10    # scaling factor
sigma_q1 = array(NA, dim = c(2, 2, 3))
sigma_q1[, , 1] = sf * matrix(c(1, 1/2, 1/2, 1), nrow = 2)
sigma_q1[, , 2] = sf * matrix(c(1, -1/2, -1/2, 1), nrow = 2)
sigma_q1[, , 3] = sf * matrix(c(3, 0, 0, 1/2), nrow = 2)

Gamma_q1 = matrix(c(0.8, 0.1, 0.1,
                    0.1, 0.8, 0.1,
                    0.1, 0.1, 0.8), nrow = 3, byrow = TRUE)

# Simulate 20 observations (Gamma symmetric, so stationary dist is uniform):
n_q1 = 20
z_q1 = numeric(n_q1)
x_q1 = matrix(NA, n_q1, 2)
for (t in 1:n_q1) {
  z_q1[t] = if (t == 1) sample(1:3, 1) else sample(1:3, 1, prob = Gamma_q1[z_q1[t - 1], ])
  x_q1[t, ] = MASS::mvrnorm(1, mu = mu_q1[z_q1[t], ], Sigma = sigma_q1[, , z_q1[t]])
}

q1_df = tibble(t = 1:n_q1, x1 = x_q1[, 1], x2 = x_q1[, 2], state = factor(z_q1))
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(data = q1_df, mapping = aes(x = x1, y = x2)) +
  geom_path(col = "grey70", arrow = arrow(length = unit(0.15, "cm"))) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = state), size = 3) +
  geom_text(aes(label = t), nudge_y = 0.9, size = 3) +
  geom_point(data = tibble(x1 = mu_q1[, 1], x2 = mu_q1[, 2]), shape = 4, size = 4, stroke = 1.5) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = state_cols) +
  labs(title = "Simulated three-state Gaussian HMM.", x = expression(x[1]), y = expression(x[2]),
       colour = "State") +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Twenty observations simulated from the three-state bivariate Gaussian HMM, coloured by the true state. The grey arrows show the time path and the crosses mark the state means.](portfolio_files/figure-pdf/fig-q1-1.pdf){#fig-q1 fig-pos='H'}
:::
:::


@fig-q1 shows the three state distributions: state 1 is centred at $(-10, 0)$ with positive correlation, state 2 at $(10, 0)$ with negative correlation and state 3 at $(0, 10)$ with a much larger variance in $x_1$ than in $x_2$. Because the diagonal of $\Gamma$ is 0.8, the process stays in a state for five steps on average ($1/(1-0.8)$), so consecutive points tend to come from the same cluster, and the path jumps between clusters only occasionally.

Given only the observations, we would need:

-   the transition probabilities: $3 \times 2 = 6$,
-   the state means: $3 \times 2 = 6$,
-   the covariance matrices: $3 \times 3 = 9$,
-   the initial distribution $\boldsymbol{\delta}$: 2 more.

That is 21 parameters (23 with $\boldsymbol{\delta}$), estimated from 20 bivariate observations (40 numbers). With so few observations the estimates would be very unreliable, and some states might not be visited often enough to estimate their covariance matrices at all.

